User-Defined Operator Symbols
==============================

Per reference §"User-Defined Operator Symbols":

Beyond the fixed built-in operators, users can introduce new binary
operator symbols by attaching `#operator("sym")` to a 2-arg trait
method. The lexer is greedy over the operator characters
`+ - * / % < > = ! & | ^ ~`, and the parser slots each user
operator into the precedence ladder by its **first character**:

| First char | Level | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| `*` `/` `%` | multiplicative | `<*` `*>` `/?` |
| `+` `-` | additive | `+++` `<+>` |
| `<` `>` `=` `!` | comparison | `<>` `<=>` `>>>` `===` `!==` |
| `&` | bitwise-and | `&&&` |
| `^` `~` | bitwise-xor | `~~` `^^` |
| `\|` | bitwise-or | `\|>` `<\|` |

This file pins:

- `<>` infix on a user struct (canonical reference example).
- Different first-char precedence: `<>` (comparison-level) vs `+++`
  (additive-level), checked by relative position in a mixed-arity
  expression.
- Symbol on the operator-char set: `~~`, `|>`, `<*>`.
- Both operands user-typed, single trait.
- Distinguishing user symbol from a reserved sigil — `++` and `--`
  are reserved, but `+++` is user-overloadable (different prefix).

File-local prefix `Ous` (Operator-Overload-User-Symbols).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features


Canonical `<>` example — set union
-----------------------------------

    struct OusSet
        bits: int

    trait OusUnion[T]
        #operator("<>")
        ous_union(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl OusUnion[OusSet]
        ous_union(a: OusSet, b: OusSet) -> OusSet = OusSet(a.bits | b.bits)

### Basic `<>` dispatch

    #test
    test_ous_union_basic() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(3)     // 0b0011
        val b = OusSet(12)    // 0b1100
        val c = a <> b
        assert(c.bits == 15, s"3 | 12 = 15, got ${c.bits}")

### Idempotence

    #test
    test_ous_union_idempotent() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(7)
        val c = a <> a
        assert(c.bits == 7, s"got ${c.bits}")


A second symbol on the comparison-level group: `<=>`
-----------------------------------------------------

    trait OusSpaceship[T]
        #operator("<=>")
        ous_cmp(a: T, b: T) -> int

    impl OusSpaceship[OusSet]
        ous_cmp(a: OusSet, b: OusSet) -> int = a.bits - b.bits

    #test
    test_ous_spaceship() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(10)
        val b = OusSet(3)
        val r = a <=> b
        assert(r == 7, s"10 - 3 = 7, got $r")


Tilde-prefix symbol: `~~`
--------------------------

`~` is in the bitwise-xor precedence group. `~~` lexes as one
operator.

    trait OusXor[T]
        #operator("~~")
        ous_xor(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl OusXor[OusSet]
        ous_xor(a: OusSet, b: OusSet) -> OusSet = OusSet(a.bits ^ b.bits)

    #test
    test_ous_xor() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(10)    // 0b1010
        val b = OusSet(6)     // 0b0110
        val c = a ~~ b
        // 0b1010 ^ 0b0110 = 0b1100 = 12
        assert(c.bits == 12, s"got ${c.bits}")


Pipe symbol: `|>`
------------------

`|` is in the bitwise-or precedence group. `|>` (compose-pipe) is
a single user operator.

    trait OusPipe[A, B, R]
        #operator("|>")
        ous_pipe(a: A, b: B) -> R

    impl OusPipe[OusSet, OusSet, OusSet]
        ous_pipe(a: OusSet, b: OusSet) -> OusSet = OusSet(a.bits | b.bits)

    #test
    test_ous_pipe() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(1)
        val b = OusSet(4)
        val c = a |> b
        assert(c.bits == 5, s"1 | 4 = 5, got ${c.bits}")


Multi-char symbol on multiplicative group: `<*>`
-------------------------------------------------

`<*>` starts with `<`. Per the reference, `<*>` lexes as one
operator. The first character is `<`, so it slots into the
**comparison** precedence group (not multiplicative — that's only for
symbols starting with `*` `/` `%`).

    trait OusApply[T]
        #operator("<*>")
        ous_apply(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl OusApply[OusSet]
        ous_apply(a: OusSet, b: OusSet) -> OusSet = OusSet(a.bits + b.bits)

    #test
    test_ous_apply_multichar() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(5)
        val b = OusSet(7)
        val c = a <*> b
        assert(c.bits == 12, s"5 + 7 = 12, got ${c.bits}")


Two user symbols composed
--------------------------

`a <> b ~~ c` — both symbols are in the bitwise group; left-to-right
associativity within the same group.

    #test
    test_ous_two_symbols_compose() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(3)     // 0b0011
        val b = OusSet(6)     // 0b0110
        val c = OusSet(10)    // 0b1010
        val r = (a <> b) ~~ c
        // a<>b = 3|6 = 7 (0b0111)
        // 7 ^ 10 (0b1010) = 0b1101 = 13
        assert(r.bits == 13, s"got ${r.bits}")


`+++` triple plus — user additive operator (different precedence
from comparison-group `<>`)
----------------------------------------------------------------

`+++` starts with `+` → additive group. `<>` starts with `<` →
comparison group. In a mixed expression, additive binds tighter than
comparison, so `a +++ b <> c` parses as `(a +++ b) <> c`.

    trait OusBigAdd[T]
        #operator("+++")
        ous_bigadd(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl OusBigAdd[OusSet]
        ous_bigadd(a: OusSet, b: OusSet) -> OusSet = OusSet(a.bits + b.bits)

    #test
    test_ous_precedence_mixed() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(2)
        val b = OusSet(3)
        val c = OusSet(0)
        // additive binds tighter: a +++ b <> c == (a +++ b) <> c
        // a +++ b = 5
        // 5 <> 0 = OusSet(5 | 0) = OusSet(5)
        val r = a +++ b <> c
        assert(r.bits == 5, s"got ${r.bits}")


Both operands user-typed via the same trait method
---------------------------------------------------

The trait above already binds two T args; this test pins that the
binding finds the impl when both operand types are equal.

    #test
    test_ous_same_type_both_sides() -> unit
        val a = OusSet(1)
        val b = OusSet(2)
        val c = a <> b
        assert(c.bits == 3, s"1 | 2 = 3, got ${c.bits}")
